--- OTHER FIELDS -------------------------------------------------------------

APPROVING PERSON — AND SAY WHY THEY MAY ACT. The distribution is an act OF THE LLC.
The consent must show who has authority to cause the LLC to act, and in what capacity:

  · Member-managed: the member manages, so the member's consent IS the LLC acting.
  · Manager-managed: the MANAGER acts. A member's consent alone may not suffice.
  · The LLC agreement may reserve distribution authority regardless of either.

Where one person holds both roles, NAME BOTH ("the sole member and manager") — the
operative authority is the management capacity, not the ownership. State the capacity;
do not cite the filing that proves it. A consent asserts its authority, it does not
argue for it, and the filings are public record. Terminology is state-specific: WA uses
GOVERNOR on the Initial Report and Annual Report; most states use MANAGER or
MANAGING MEMBER. Pull the filed report and use the term that appears on it.

Draft the operative line so the LLC is the actor — "<PERSON>, as <CAPACITY>, causes the
Company to distribute" — not "<PERSON> resolves to take the Company's cash."

DO NOT put ledger accounts or journal-entry numbers in the signed consent. The books
cite the consent as their authority; a signed document citing the books inverts that,
and goes stale the moment the chart of accounts is renumbered. Book the entry in
`books/journal-entries.md` and let it reference this document.

LLC AGREEMENT. Nearly every act conditions distributions on the agreement first. If one
exists, READ IT and confirm it permits this. If none exists, say so as a documented
search result — never "the member is not aware of any," which does not cure an
agreement that exists and restricts.

FIGURES. Use the position on the TRANSFER date, not a stale feed pull. Confirm the
liabilities figure is complete: open tax notices, assessments, unbilled professional
fees, and chargebacks are liabilities even when unquantified.

--- FOR A DISREGARDED SMLLC --------------------------------------------------

FEDERAL. A transfer between a disregarded LLC and its sole owner is a NON-EVENT
(Treas. Reg. §301.7701-3): no income, no deduction, nothing reportable. It remains a
real legal and bookkeeping transaction. Keep that analysis in the books, not here.

STATE MAY DIVERGE. A disregarded entity can still be a SEPARATE taxpayer for state
gross-receipts, excise, or franchise tax. A genuine distribution on an ownership
interest is generally not gross receipts — confirm against the state's own definition
rather than assuming federal disregard carries over.

ACCOUNTS. Entry mechanics, the parent/SMLLC account mapping, and the consolidation
eliminations live in `entities/disregarded.md`. Do not restate them here.

--- EXECUTION ----------------------------------------------------------------

TWO TRANSFERS, NEVER ONE. The distribution goes to the MEMBER'S OWN ACCOUNT; the
member then pays its own obligations from that account as a separate transaction on a
later date. The Company paying the Member's creditors directly is commingling and a
veil-piercing factor. This is operating discipline, not a clause — which is why it is
not a resolution above.

ONE CONSENT PER DISTRIBUTION. A single consent is not standing authority for a series
of transfers; a blanket authorization for ongoing sweeps reads as exactly the
commingling this document exists to rebut.
